This implementation uses two queues to simulate a stack. The `push` operation moves all elements from the first queue (`q1`) to the second queue (`q2`), then adds the new element to the front of `q2`. This ensures that the newest element is always at the front of `q2`, which represents the top of the stack.

The `pop` operation removes the last element from `q1`, which represents the top of the stack. If `q1` is empty, it means there are no elements in the stack and an underflow condition occurs.

The `top` operation returns the top element of the stack, which is the front element of `q1`.

The `empty` method checks if the stack is empty by checking if `q1` has any elements. The `size` method returns the number of elements in `q1`, which represents the size of the stack.

